{"case_id": "mathdial-001", "mathdial_qid": 5000014, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Four classmates were comparing their ages based on their birth month. They found out that Jolyn is 2 months older than Therese while Therese is 5 months older than Aivo. Then, Leon is 2 months older than Aivo. How much older in months is Jolyn than Leon?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nWe can start by creating a diagram to visualize the relationships between the classmates' ages:\n\nAivo --(5 months)--> Therese --(2 months)--> Jolyn\n|\n(2 months)\n|\nLeon\n\nFrom the diagram, we can see that Jolyn is 2 + 5 = 7 months older than Aivo, and Leon is 2 months older than Aivo. Therefore, Jolyn is 7 + 2 = 9 months older than Leon.\n 9\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Four classmates were comparing their ages based on their birth month. They found out that Jolyn is 2 months older than Therese while Therese is 5 months older than Aivo. Then, Leon is 2 months older than Aivo. How much older in months is Jolyn than Leon?", "expected_answer": "5", "student_answer": "9", "student_confusion": "they had correctly worked out the first part of the calculation but had then added instead of subtracting for the second part", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 2}
{"case_id": "mathdial-002", "mathdial_qid": 5000018, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Hector purchased a container of gumballs.  He gave 4 to Todd, then he gave twice as many as he had given Todd to Alisha, and then he gave 5 less than four times as many to Bobby as he had given to Alisha.  If Hector had 6 gumballs remaining, what is the total number of gumballs that Hector purchased?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nLet's work backwards to find out how many gumballs Hector purchased.\nIf Hector had 6 gumballs remaining, then he gave Bobby 4(2) - 5 = 3 gumballs.\nSo, Hector gave Alisha 2 x 4 = 8 gumballs.\nAfter giving Todd 4 gumballs, Hector would have had 6 + 4 + 8 + 3 = 21 gumballs initially.\nTherefore, Hector purchased 21 gumballs.\n 21\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Hector purchased a container of gumballs.  He gave 4 to Todd, then he gave twice as many as he had given Todd to Alisha, and then he gave 5 less than four times as many to Bobby as he had given to Alisha.  If Hector had 6 gumballs remaining, what is the total number of gumballs that Hector purchased?", "expected_answer": "45", "student_answer": "21", "student_confusion": "Student left out a step to get the correct number of gumballs one person received.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 2}
{"case_id": "mathdial-003", "mathdial_qid": 5000019, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "A craft store makes a third of its sales in the fabric section, a quarter of its sales in the jewelry section, and the rest in the stationery section. They made 36 sales today. How many sales were in the stationery section?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThe fraction of sales in the fabric section is 1/3, and the fraction of sales in the jewelry section is 1/4. So the fraction of sales in the stationery section is:\n1 - 1/3 - 1/4 = 4/12 - 4/12 - 3/12 = 1/12\nIf 1/12 of the sales were in the stationery section, and there were 36 sales in total, then the number of sales in the stationery section is:\n1/12 x 36 = 3\n 3\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "A craft store makes a third of its sales in the fabric section, a quarter of its sales in the jewelry section, and the rest in the stationery section. They made 36 sales today. How many sales were in the stationery section?", "expected_answer": "15", "student_answer": "3", "student_confusion": "the student was making a numerical error in multiplying and dividing fractions. the method used to solve the problem was correctly identified.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-004", "mathdial_qid": 5000027, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Janice's office is on the third floor, and she has to walk up 3 flights of stairs to get to her office.  In a single day, she goes up the three flights of stairs 5 times, down the the three flights of stairs 3 times, and uses the elevator the remainder of the time.  How many flights of stairs does Janice walk (up and down) in a single day?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nIn a single trip to her office, Janice walks up and down 3 flights of stairs, for a total of 3+3=6 flights of stairs.\nIn a day, Janice walks up and down 6 x 5 = 30 flights of stairs.\nShe also walks down 6 x 3 = 18 flights of stairs when she goes home.\nTherefore, Janice walks a total of 30 + 18 = 48 flights of stairs in a single day.\n 48\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Janice's office is on the third floor, and she has to walk up 3 flights of stairs to get to her office.  In a single day, she goes up the three flights of stairs 5 times, down the the three flights of stairs 3 times, and uses the elevator the remainder of the time.  How many flights of stairs does Janice walk (up and down) in a single day?", "expected_answer": "24", "student_answer": "48", "student_confusion": "Student thought the worker went up and down stairs, when in fact they just went up.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-005", "mathdial_qid": 5000057, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "At a gathering, it was reported that 26 people took wine, 22 people took soda, and 17 people took both drinks. If each person could have taken one or more drinks regardless of what was reported, how many people altogether were at the gathering?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThe total number of people who took either wine or soda is 26 + 22 = 48.\nBut we have double-counted the 17 people who took both drinks, so we need to subtract them once: 48 - 17 = 31.\nTherefore, there were 31 people who took only wine or only soda, and there were also 17 people who took both drinks.\nSo the total number of people at the gathering is 31 + 17 = 48.\n 48\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "At a gathering, it was reported that 26 people took wine, 22 people took soda, and 17 people took both drinks. If each person could have taken one or more drinks regardless of what was reported, how many people altogether were at the gathering?", "expected_answer": "31", "student_answer": "48", "student_confusion": "the student got the correct answer by subtracting the 17 people who were double counted. but she added 17 once again thus nullifying the original subtraction.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-006", "mathdial_qid": 5000061, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Carl has a jar full of marbles. He takes out 12 marbles to play a game with, but he accidentally drops them and 1/2 the marbles get lost. So Carl takes out 10 more marbles. While he is playing his game his mother comes home with another bag of marbles for him, which has 25 marbles in it. If Carl can't find his lost marbles, then how many marbles is he going to put in the jar after he plays his game, from both his original marbles and the new ones?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nLet's start by finding out how many marbles Carl had originally.\nHe took out 12 marbles and lost 1/2 of them, which means he lost 12/2 = 6 marbles.\nSo, originally he had 12 + 6 = 18 marbles.\nThen he takes out 10 more marbles, leaving him with 18 - 10 = 8 marbles.\nFinally, his mother brings 25 more marbles, so in total Carl has 8 + 25 = 33 marbles.\n 33\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Carl has a jar full of marbles. He takes out 12 marbles to play a game with, but he accidentally drops them and 1/2 the marbles get lost. So Carl takes out 10 more marbles. While he is playing his game his mother comes home with another bag of marbles for him, which has 25 marbles in it. If Carl can't find his lost marbles, then how many marbles is he going to put in the jar after he plays his game, from both his original marbles and the new ones?", "expected_answer": "41", "student_answer": "33", "student_confusion": "they mixed up addion and subtraction", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-007", "mathdial_qid": 5000079, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "A man is returning home from work and trying to decide which route to take.  His first route option includes 3 stoplights.  This route will take him 10 minutes if all three lights are green, but each light that is red will add 3 minutes to the trip.  The second route does not include any stoplights and takes 14 minutes.  If the man chooses the first route, how much longer will his trip be if all 3 stoplights are red?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nIf all three lights are green, the trip will take 10 minutes.\nIf one light is red, the trip will take 10 + 3 = 13 minutes.\nIf two lights are red, the trip will take 10 + 3 + 3 = 16 minutes.\nIf all three lights are red, the trip will take 10 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 19 minutes.\nSo, if all three stoplights are red, the trip will be 19 - 10 = 9 minutes longer.\n 9\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "A man is returning home from work and trying to decide which route to take.  His first route option includes 3 stoplights.  This route will take him 10 minutes if all three lights are green, but each light that is red will add 3 minutes to the trip.  The second route does not include any stoplights and takes 14 minutes.  If the man chooses the first route, how much longer will his trip be if all 3 stoplights are red?", "expected_answer": "5", "student_answer": "9", "student_confusion": "student calculated the difference in time between the first route in two scenarios instead of calculating the difference in route 1 and 2.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 2}
{"case_id": "mathdial-008", "mathdial_qid": 5000082, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Earl started delivering newspapers on the first floor of a condominium building. He then went up 5 floors then went down 2 floors. He again went up 7 floors and found that he is 9 floors away from the top of the building. How many floors does the building have?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nEarl went up 5 floors and then down 2 floors, so he ended up 5 - 2 = 3 floors higher than his starting point.\nHe then went up 7 floors, so he was 3 + 7 = 10 floors higher than his starting point.\nIf he was 9 floors away from the top of the building, then the building has 10 + 9 = 19 floors in total.\n 19\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Earl started delivering newspapers on the first floor of a condominium building. He then went up 5 floors then went down 2 floors. He again went up 7 floors and found that he is 9 floors away from the top of the building. How many floors does the building have?", "expected_answer": "20", "student_answer": "19", "student_confusion": "Didn't add the first floor", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
{"case_id": "mathdial-009", "mathdial_qid": 5000124, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "The county fair was hosting a pumpkin contest to see who could grow the biggest pumpkin in pounds.  Brad entered his pumpkin with a weight of 54 pounds.  Jessica's was half the weight of Brad's.  Betty's pumpkin weight 4 times the amount of Jessica's pumpkin.  What is the difference between the heaviest and lightest pumpkin in  pounds?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nJessica's pumpkin weighed half of Brad's, which is 54/2 = 27 pounds.\nBetty's pumpkin weighed 4 times the amount of Jessica's, which is 4 x 27 = 108 pounds.\nThe heaviest pumpkin is Betty's at 108 pounds and the lightest is Brad's at 54 pounds.\nThe difference between them is 108 - 54 = 54 pounds.\n 54\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "The county fair was hosting a pumpkin contest to see who could grow the biggest pumpkin in pounds.  Brad entered his pumpkin with a weight of 54 pounds.  Jessica's was half the weight of Brad's.  Betty's pumpkin weight 4 times the amount of Jessica's pumpkin.  What is the difference between the heaviest and lightest pumpkin in  pounds?", "expected_answer": "81", "student_answer": "54", "student_confusion": "Student had knowledge pertaining to 'range' but accidently forgot about the lowest number", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-010", "mathdial_qid": 5000130, "grade": 2, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Carla is taking attendance for her sixth-grade class. She knows that 2 students are in the restroom, and one less than three times that number of students are absent. If there are four rows with six desks each that are all 2/3 full, how many students does Carla teach, including the ones in and out of the classroom?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nNumber of students in the restroom = 2\nNumber of absent students = 3(2) - 1 = 5\nNumber of students present = Total number of students - (number of students in restroom + number of absent students)\nNumber of students present in each row = 6 x 2/3 = 4\nTotal number of students in all rows = 4 x 4 = 16\nTotal number of students Carla teaches = number of students present + number of students in the restroom = 16 + 2 = 18\n 18\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Carla is taking attendance for her sixth-grade class. She knows that 2 students are in the restroom, and one less than three times that number of students are absent. If there are four rows with six desks each that are all 2/3 full, how many students does Carla teach, including the ones in and out of the classroom?", "expected_answer": "23", "student_answer": "18", "student_confusion": "They were adding the numbers wrong", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 1}
{"case_id": "mathdial-011", "mathdial_qid": 5000134, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Jeff committed to run for an hour a day during weekdays. On Thursday, he cut short his run by 20 minutes but was able to jog 10 minutes more on Friday. How many minutes was he able to run for that week?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nJeff runs for 1 hour x 5 days = 5 hours from Monday to Friday.\nHe cut short his Thursday run by 20 minutes, so he only ran for 1 hour - 20 minutes = 40 minutes.\nHe was able to jog for 1 hour + 10 minutes = 70 minutes on Friday.\nSo, he was able to run for a total of 5 hours x 60 minutes + 40 minutes + 70 minutes = 410 minutes that week.\n 410\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Jeff committed to run for an hour a day during weekdays. On Thursday, he cut short his run by 20 minutes but was able to jog 10 minutes more on Friday. How many minutes was he able to run for that week?", "expected_answer": "290", "student_answer": "410", "student_confusion": "", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
{"case_id": "mathdial-012", "mathdial_qid": 5000138, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Jake is retrieving the notes on a communal board, putting all of the red notes in the complaints bin and all of the blue notes in the compliments bin. The red notes were in 5 rows with 6 notes in each row. There are 2 blue notes under each of the red notes, as well as another 10 blue notes scattered at the bottom of the board. How many notes in total are put into the complaints and compliments bins?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThere are 5 x 6 = 30 red notes.\nSo there are 30 x 2 = 60 blue notes that were under the red notes.\nTherefore the total number of blue notes is 60 + 10 = 70.\nAnd the total number of notes put into the complaints and compliments bins is 30 + 60 = 90.\n 90\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Jake is retrieving the notes on a communal board, putting all of the red notes in the complaints bin and all of the blue notes in the compliments bin. The red notes were in 5 rows with 6 notes in each row. There are 2 blue notes under each of the red notes, as well as another 10 blue notes scattered at the bottom of the board. How many notes in total are put into the complaints and compliments bins?", "expected_answer": "100", "student_answer": "90", "student_confusion": "Student wrote down the wrong amount.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 1}
{"case_id": "mathdial-013", "mathdial_qid": 5000155, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Kevin holds the world record for eating the biggest quantity of hot wings in 8 minutes. He can eat 64 wings without stopping. Alan, a boy who loves hot wings, wants to beat Kevin's record.  He is currently able to eat 5 hot wings per minute. How many more wings must he eat per minute to beat Kevin's record?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nKevin can eat 64/8 = 8 hot wings per minute.\nTo beat Kevin's record, Alan needs to eat more than 8 hot wings per minute.\nHe needs to eat 64 hot wings / 8 minutes = 8 hot wings per minute to match Kevin's record.\nSo, Alan needs to eat 8 - 5 = 3 more hot wings per minute to beat Kevin's record.\n 3\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Kevin holds the world record for eating the biggest quantity of hot wings in 8 minutes. He can eat 64 wings without stopping. Alan, a boy who loves hot wings, wants to beat Kevin's record.  He is currently able to eat 5 hot wings per minute. How many more wings must he eat per minute to beat Kevin's record?", "expected_answer": "4", "student_answer": "3", "student_confusion": "The student was overcomplicating the math in the problem", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-014", "mathdial_qid": 5000161, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Ittymangnark and Kingnook are an Eskimo couple living in the most northern region of the Alaskan wilderness.  Together, they live with their child, Oomyapeck.  Every day Ittymangnark catches enough fish for the three of them to eat for the day and they split the fish equally between the three of them.  But after they have split the fish, they give Oomyapeck all of the eyes, who gives two of the eyes to his dog and eats the rest himself.  How many fish will each of them be given to eat if Oomyapeck eats 22 eyes in a day?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nSince Oomyapeck eats all the eyes from each fish, it means they split the fish without the eyes. So, the total number of eyes in all the fish caught is 22 x 3 = 66.\nSince each fish has two eyes, the number of fish caught is 66/2 = 33.\nTherefore, each of them will be given 33/3 = 11 fish to eat.\n 11\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Ittymangnark and Kingnook are an Eskimo couple living in the most northern region of the Alaskan wilderness.  Together, they live with their child, Oomyapeck.  Every day Ittymangnark catches enough fish for the three of them to eat for the day and they split the fish equally between the three of them.  But after they have split the fish, they give Oomyapeck all of the eyes, who gives two of the eyes to his dog and eats the rest himself.  How many fish will each of them be given to eat if Oomyapeck eats 22 eyes in a day?", "expected_answer": "4", "student_answer": "11", "student_confusion": "student miscalculated the number of eyes in the first step", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-015", "mathdial_qid": 5000164, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Kamil wants to renovate his kitchen at home. For this purpose, he hired two professionals who work for him 6 hours a day for 7 days. What does it cost Kamil to hire these professionals if one of them is paid $15 per hour of work?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThe two professionals work together for 6 x 2 = 12 hours a day.\nThey work for a total of 12 x 7 = 84 hours in 7 days.\nSo, the first professional earns a total of 15 x 84 = $1260 for the entire work.\nSince there are two professionals, Kamil pays a total of 1260 x 2 = $2520 for their entire work.\n 2520\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Kamil wants to renovate his kitchen at home. For this purpose, he hired two professionals who work for him 6 hours a day for 7 days. What does it cost Kamil to hire these professionals if one of them is paid $15 per hour of work?", "expected_answer": "1260", "student_answer": "2520", "student_confusion": "Repetitive multiplication in the last step", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 2}
{"case_id": "mathdial-016", "mathdial_qid": 5000182, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "The difference in the number of pens that Alice and Clara each have matches their age difference. Alice has 60 pens and Clara has 2/5 times as many pens as Alice. If Alice's age is 20 and Clara is older than Alice, calculate Clara's age in 5 years to come.\n\nThis is how I did it:\nIf Alice has 60 pens, and Clara has 2/5 times as many pens as Alice, Clara has 2/5*60 = 24 pens.\nThe age difference between Alice and Clara is 24, given that the difference in the number of pens that Alice and Clara each have matches their age difference.\nIf Alice is 20 years old, Clara is 24+20 = 44 years old.\nIn five years, Clara will be 44+5 = 49 years old.\n 49\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "The difference in the number of pens that Alice and Clara each have matches their age difference. Alice has 60 pens and Clara has 2/5 times as many pens as Alice. If Alice's age is 20 and Clara is older than Alice, calculate Clara's age in 5 years to come.", "expected_answer": "61", "student_answer": "49", "student_confusion": "Difficulty in identification of differences in the problem", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4, "beyond_class_5_note": "a division that does not come out whole"}
{"case_id": "mathdial-017", "mathdial_qid": 5000185, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Jay has decided to save money from his paycheck every week. He has decided that he will increase the amount he saves each week by 10 dollars. If he started by saving 20 dollars this week, how much will he have saved in a month from now?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nIn one week, Jay saves 20 dollars.\nIn the second week, he saves 20+10 = 30 dollars.\nIn the third week, he saves 30+10 = 40 dollars.\nIn the fourth week, he saves 40+10 = 50 dollars.\nSo in a month, which is four weeks, Jay saves 20+30+40+50 = 140 dollars.\n 140\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Jay has decided to save money from his paycheck every week. He has decided that he will increase the amount he saves each week by 10 dollars. If he started by saving 20 dollars this week, how much will he have saved in a month from now?", "expected_answer": "60", "student_answer": "140", "student_confusion": "they are adding up all the weeks and the initial 20 dollars to find the total", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
{"case_id": "mathdial-018", "mathdial_qid": 5000194, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Jack will have ten times more handball trophies than Michael has right now in three years. If Michael has 30 trophies right now, and the number of his trophies increases by 100 in three years, what's the total number of trophies they'll have altogether after three years?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nIn three years, Michael will have 30+100 = 130 trophies.\nJack will have ten times more handball trophies than Michael has right now in three years, meaning Jack will have 10*130 = 1300 handball trophies in three years.\nAltogether, they will have 1300+130 = 1430 trophies in three years.\n 1430\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Jack will have ten times more handball trophies than Michael has right now in three years. If Michael has 30 trophies right now, and the number of his trophies increases by 100 in three years, what's the total number of trophies they'll have altogether after three years?", "expected_answer": "430", "student_answer": "1430", "student_confusion": "The student multiplied numbers incorrectly", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-019", "mathdial_qid": 5000206, "grade": 5, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "A monitor is 21 inches by 12 inches.  There are 100 dots per inch.  How many total pixels are there?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThe total number of pixels is found by multiplying the number of dots per inch (dpi) by the total area of the monitor. The total area of the monitor is 21 x 12 = 252 square inches. \n\nSo the total number of pixels is 100 dpi x 252 square inches = 25,200 pixels. \n\n 25,200\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "A monitor is 21 inches by 12 inches.  There are 100 dots per inch.  How many total pixels are there?", "expected_answer": "2520000", "student_answer": "25200", "student_confusion": "They misread the calculator", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-020", "mathdial_qid": 5000212, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Frank is walking through a corn maze. He has already spent 45 minutes inside. He has done 4 other corn mazes and finished those in 50 minutes on average. How much longer can he spend inside if he wants to ensure that his average doesn't go above 60 minutes per maze?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nFrank has completed a total of 5 corn mazes (including the current one), so he has spent a total of 45 + 50(4) = 245 minutes in the mazes.\nTo keep his average below 60 minutes per maze, he can spend a total of 60(5) - 245 = 55 minutes in the current maze.\nSince he has already spent 45 minutes inside, he can spend an additional 55 - 45 = 10 minutes inside.\n 10\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Frank is walking through a corn maze. He has already spent 45 minutes inside. He has done 4 other corn mazes and finished those in 50 minutes on average. How much longer can he spend inside if he wants to ensure that his average doesn't go above 60 minutes per maze?", "expected_answer": "55", "student_answer": "10", "student_confusion": "The student was not taking into account the initial 300. With the help of a calculator he got to the correct solution", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
{"case_id": "mathdial-021", "mathdial_qid": 5000231, "grade": 5, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Denny, an Instagram influencer, has 100000 followers and gets 1000 new followers every day. How many followers will he have if 20000 people unfollowed him in a year?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nIn a day, Denny's followers increase to 100000 + 1000 = 101000.\nIn a year, there are 365 days.\nSo, the total increase in followers in a year is 101000 x 365 = 36865000.\nIf 20000 people unfollowed him in a year, then the total number of followers he would have is 36865000 - 20000 = 36845000.\n 36845000\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Denny, an Instagram influencer, has 100000 followers and gets 1000 new followers every day. How many followers will he have if 20000 people unfollowed him in a year?", "expected_answer": "445000", "student_answer": "36845000", "student_confusion": "The student was making an initial first error . This caused the rest of the problem to be incorrect", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-022", "mathdial_qid": 5000234, "grade": 5, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "During the outbreak of the coronavirus, a company had to ramp up its toilet paper production three times more to cover the increased demand. If the company was producing 7000 toilet paper per day, calculate its total toilet paper production during March of 2020 after increasing its production.\n\nThis is how I did it:\nIf the company ramped up its toilet paper production three times more, it means it increased production by 3 times the original production of 7000, which is 3*7000 = 21000 toilet papers per day.\nIn March of 2020, there were 31 days, so the total toilet paper production for that month would be 31*21000 = 651000 toilet papers.\n 651000\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "During the outbreak of the coronavirus, a company had to ramp up its toilet paper production three times more to cover the increased demand. If the company was producing 7000 toilet paper per day, calculate its total toilet paper production during March of 2020 after increasing its production.", "expected_answer": "868000", "student_answer": "651000", "student_confusion": "multiplication error", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-023", "mathdial_qid": 5000238, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Tammy just got her hair cut. For every 14 haircuts, she gets a free additional haircut. She has gotten 5 free haircuts already. She is 5 haircuts away from another free one. How many haircuts has she gotten there?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nTammy has received a total of 5 + 1 = 6 free haircuts.\nSince she gets a free haircut for every 14 haircuts, she has received a total of 6 x 14 = 84 haircuts.\nAdding the 5 haircuts she is away from another free one, Tammy has gotten a total of 84 + 5 = 89 haircuts. \n 89\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Tammy just got her hair cut. For every 14 haircuts, she gets a free additional haircut. She has gotten 5 free haircuts already. She is 5 haircuts away from another free one. How many haircuts has she gotten there?", "expected_answer": "79", "student_answer": "89", "student_confusion": "repetitive error", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-024", "mathdial_qid": 5000240, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "A taco truck is selling soft tacos for $2 and hard shell tacos for $5 during the lunch rush. The first group of customers is a family that buys four hard shell tacos and three soft tacos. The rest of the customers in the lunch rush only buy two soft tacos each. There were ten customers after the family. How many dollars did the taco truck make during the lunch rush?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThe family spent 4 x $5 = $20 on hard shell tacos.\nThe family spent 3 x $2 = $6 on soft tacos.\nThe rest of the customers bought 2 x $2 = $4 worth of soft tacos each.\nThere were 10 - 1 = 9 customers after the family.\nThe rest of the customers spent 9 x $4 = $36 on soft tacos.\nThe taco truck made $20 + $6 + $36 = $62 during the lunch rush.\n 62\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "A taco truck is selling soft tacos for $2 and hard shell tacos for $5 during the lunch rush. The first group of customers is a family that buys four hard shell tacos and three soft tacos. The rest of the customers in the lunch rush only buy two soft tacos each. There were ten customers after the family. How many dollars did the taco truck make during the lunch rush?", "expected_answer": "66", "student_answer": "62", "student_confusion": "Irrelevant subtraction in the step of calculating customers after the family.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 1}
{"case_id": "mathdial-025", "mathdial_qid": 5000290, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Mr. Langsley commutes to work every day by bus. The bus picks him up at 6:00 a.m. and takes forty minutes to arrive at the first station. If Mr. Langsley arrives at work at 9:00 a.m., what's the total time taken in minutes from the first station to Mr. Langsley's workplace?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nMr. Langsley spends 9:00 a.m. - 6:00 a.m. = 3 hours commuting.\nSince the bus takes forty minutes to arrive at the first station, Mr. Langsley spends 3 hours x 60 minutes + 40 minutes = 220 minutes commuting.\nThe total time taken from the first station to Mr. Langsley's workplace is 220 minutes - 40 minutes = 180 minutes.\n 180\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Mr. Langsley commutes to work every day by bus. The bus picks him up at 6:00 a.m. and takes forty minutes to arrive at the first station. If Mr. Langsley arrives at work at 9:00 a.m., what's the total time taken in minutes from the first station to Mr. Langsley's workplace?", "expected_answer": "140", "student_answer": "180", "student_confusion": "Michael was adding the first journey onto the overall 3 hour time limit.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 2}
{"case_id": "mathdial-026", "mathdial_qid": 5000292, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Drew is reseeding his lawn with grass seed. One bag of grass seed covers 250 square feet of lawn. His lawn is 22 feet from the house to the curb and 36 feet from side to side. He bought four bags of seed. How many extra square feet could the leftover grass seed cover after Drew reseeds his lawn?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThe area of Drew's lawn is 22 x 36 = 792 square feet.\nFour bags of grass seed can cover 4 x 250 = 1000 square feet.\nDrew will use all the grass seed to cover his lawn, so there won't be any leftover to cover extra square feet.\nTherefore, the leftover grass seed can't cover any extra square feet.\n 0\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Drew is reseeding his lawn with grass seed. One bag of grass seed covers 250 square feet of lawn. His lawn is 22 feet from the house to the curb and 36 feet from side to side. He bought four bags of seed. How many extra square feet could the leftover grass seed cover after Drew reseeds his lawn?", "expected_answer": "208", "student_answer": "0", "student_confusion": "She did the correct maths but didn't read the question scarefully enough.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-027", "mathdial_qid": 5000293, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Kameron has 100 kangaroos on his large farm; Bert has 20 kangaroos on his farm. In how many more days will Bert have the same number of kangaroos as Kameron does now if he buys kangaroos at the same rate of 2 new kangaroos per day?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThe difference in the number of kangaroos is 100 - 20 = 80.\nBert needs to buy 80/2 = 40 kangaroos to catch up with Kameron.\nAt a rate of 2 new kangaroos per day, Bert will catch up in 40/2 = 20 days.\nTherefore, Bert will have the same number of kangaroos as Kameron does now in 20 days.\n 20\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Kameron has 100 kangaroos on his large farm; Bert has 20 kangaroos on his farm. In how many more days will Bert have the same number of kangaroos as Kameron does now if he buys kangaroos at the same rate of 2 new kangaroos per day?", "expected_answer": "40", "student_answer": "20", "student_confusion": "The student didn't understand what each number represented and couldn't seem to identify it.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 2}
{"case_id": "mathdial-028", "mathdial_qid": 5000298, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Carl is hosting an open house for his new business.  He knows 50 people will show up and hopes that another 40 people will randomly show up.  He’s created 10 extravagant gift bags for the first 10 people who visit his shop.  He’s made average gift bags for 20 people but needs to make enough for everyone who visits to have one.  How many more bags does he need to make?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nCarl is hoping for a total of 50 + 40 = 90 people to show up.\nHe has already made 10 gift bags, so he still needs to make 90 - 10 = 80 gift bags.\nHe has made 10 extravagant gift bags and 20 average gift bags, for a total of 10 + 20 = 30 gift bags.\nThus, he needs to make 80 - 30 = 50 more gift bags.\n 50\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Carl is hosting an open house for his new business.  He knows 50 people will show up and hopes that another 40 people will randomly show up.  He’s created 10 extravagant gift bags for the first 10 people who visit his shop.  He’s made average gift bags for 20 people but needs to make enough for everyone who visits to have one.  How many more bags does he need to make?", "expected_answer": "60", "student_answer": "50", "student_confusion": "i'm not sure. Stephanie seemed stuck on 50, it would help to see her workings", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
