{"case_id": "mathdial-030", "mathdial_qid": 5000363, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Marcus takes a deck of standard playing cards and takes out all the face cards and the 8's. Mark picks a card at random and then replaces it 36 times. How many times should he expect to pick a card that's both red and has a number divisible by 3?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThere are 9 cards that are red and have a number divisible by 3: 3 of hearts, 3 of diamonds, 6 of hearts, 6 of diamonds, 9 of hearts, 9 of diamonds, 3 of clubs, 6 of clubs, and 9 of clubs.\nOut of the remaining 36 cards, half are red and half are black, so there are 18 red cards.\nThe probability of picking a red card that's divisible by 3 is 9/36 = 1/4.\nTherefore, Mark can expect to pick such a card 1/4 x 36 = 9 times.\n 9\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Marcus takes a deck of standard playing cards and takes out all the face cards and the 8's. Mark picks a card at random and then replaces it 36 times. How many times should he expect to pick a card that's both red and has a number divisible by 3?", "expected_answer": "6", "student_answer": "9", "student_confusion": "Didn't understand the question correctly.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 1, "beyond_class_5_note": "a division that does not come out whole"}
{"case_id": "mathdial-031", "mathdial_qid": 5000373, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "A bird is building a nest from twigs. The bird has put twelve twigs together already to make a circle. For each twig it placed into the circle, it wants to weave in six more twigs to fill out the nest. It knows a tree that has dropped a third of the twigs it needs. How many twigs will the bird still need to find to finish its nest?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThe bird originally used 12 twigs for the nest.\nIt wants to add 6 twigs for each of the 12 twigs, so it needs 6 x 12 = 72 more twigs to finish the nest.\nIf the tree has dropped one-third of the twigs needed, then it has dropped 1/3 x (12 + 72) = 28 twigs.\nTherefore, the bird still needs 12 + 72 - 28 = 56 twigs to finish its nest.\n 56\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "A bird is building a nest from twigs. The bird has put twelve twigs together already to make a circle. For each twig it placed into the circle, it wants to weave in six more twigs to fill out the nest. It knows a tree that has dropped a third of the twigs it needs. How many twigs will the bird still need to find to finish its nest?", "expected_answer": "48", "student_answer": "56", "student_confusion": "student had calculated 1/3 of the total number of twigs required to build a nest, instead of 1/3 of 72 twigs required to fill the circle.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-032", "mathdial_qid": 5000379, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Lee is saving up for a new pair of sneakers which cost $90. He already has $15 saved. He plans to sell his old action figures to make the rest. If he sells 10 action figures and still has $25 left after buying the sneakers how much did he charge per action figure?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nLee needs to save $90 - $15 = $75 for the sneakers.\nAfter selling the action figures, he has $25 + the money he gets from selling the action figures = $75.\nSo he earned $75 - $25 = $50 from selling the action figures.\nSince he sold 10 action figures, he charged $50/10 = $5 per action figure.\n 5\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Lee is saving up for a new pair of sneakers which cost $90. He already has $15 saved. He plans to sell his old action figures to make the rest. If he sells 10 action figures and still has $25 left after buying the sneakers how much did he charge per action figure?", "expected_answer": "10", "student_answer": "5", "student_confusion": "repetitive numerical error", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
