{"case_id": "mathdial-036", "mathdial_qid": 5000483, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "While planning to finish reading a 140-page book in one week, Jessy initially decides to read 3 times daily, 6 pages each time, every day of the week. How many more pages should she read per day to actually achieve her goal?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nJessy initially planned to read 3 x 6 = 18 pages per day.\nIn one week, she would read a total of 18 x 7 = 126 pages.\nTo reach her goal, she needs to read an additional 140 - 126 = 14 pages.\nTo achieve this, she needs to read an additional 14/7 = 2 pages per day.\nTherefore, Jessy needs to read 18 + 2 = 20 pages per day to achieve her goal.\n 20\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "While planning to finish reading a 140-page book in one week, Jessy initially decides to read 3 times daily, 6 pages each time, every day of the week. How many more pages should she read per day to actually achieve her goal?", "expected_answer": "2", "student_answer": "20", "student_confusion": "Mis read the question and therefore did the wrong calculation.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
