{"case_id": "mathdial-042", "mathdial_qid": 5000523, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Lilibeth and her friends go strawberry picking. Lilibeth fills 6 baskets where each basket holds 50 strawberries. If three of Lilibeth's friends pick the same amount as her, how many strawberries do Lilibeth and her friends pick in all?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nLilibeth picked a total of 6 x 50 = 300 strawberries.\nThree of Lilibeth's friends also picked 300 strawberries each, so together they picked a total of 300 x 4 = 1200 strawberries.\nLilibeth and her friends picked 300 + 1200 = 1500 strawberries in all.\n 1500\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Lilibeth and her friends go strawberry picking. Lilibeth fills 6 baskets where each basket holds 50 strawberries. If three of Lilibeth's friends pick the same amount as her, how many strawberries do Lilibeth and her friends pick in all?", "expected_answer": "1200", "student_answer": "1500", "student_confusion": "student double counted one of the people mentioned in the question", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-043", "mathdial_qid": 5000541, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Grayson has 100 questions on a test. He answers some of the questions for 2 hours, taking two minutes for each answer, then realizes he doesn't know the rest of the questions. How many questions does Grayson leave unanswered when he submits his test?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nGrayson has 2 x 60 = 120 minutes to answer the test questions.\nHe spent 2 x 60/2 = 60 minutes answering some of the questions.\nHe spent 120 - 60 = 60 minutes not answering the remaining questions.\nHe could have answered 60/2 = 30 questions in the remaining time.\nTherefore, Grayson leaves 100 - 30 = 70 questions unanswered. \n 70\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Grayson has 100 questions on a test. He answers some of the questions for 2 hours, taking two minutes for each answer, then realizes he doesn't know the rest of the questions. How many questions does Grayson leave unanswered when he submits his test?", "expected_answer": "40", "student_answer": "70", "student_confusion": "The student was trying to work out how many minutes rather than the number of questions", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-044", "mathdial_qid": 5000580, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "On Black Friday a popular electronics store sells 327 televisions. This is 50 more than they sold last Black Friday. If this sales trend continues and they sell 50 more televisions each year for three years, how many televisions will they sell on Black Friday three years from now?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nOn the current Black Friday, the store sold 327 televisions, which is 50 more than last year. So last year they sold 327 - 50 = 277 televisions.\nIf they sell 50 more televisions each year for three years, the number of televisions sold in the next three years will be:\nYear 1: 327\nYear 2: 327 + 50 = 377\nYear 3: 377 + 50 = 427\nSo, they will sell 427 televisions on Black Friday three years from now.\n 427\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "On Black Friday a popular electronics store sells 327 televisions. This is 50 more than they sold last Black Friday. If this sales trend continues and they sell 50 more televisions each year for three years, how many televisions will they sell on Black Friday three years from now?", "expected_answer": "477", "student_answer": "427", "student_confusion": "The student had subtracted the initial 50 TVs", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 2}
{"case_id": "mathdial-045", "mathdial_qid": 5000588, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Peter and Kristin are to read 20 fantasy novels each in a week. Peter can read three times as fast as Kristin. If Peter reads one book in 18 hours, how long will Kristin read half of her books?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nPeter can read one book in 18 hours, so Kristin can read one book in 18 x 3 = 54 hours.\nKristin needs to read half of her books, which is 20/2 = 10 books.\nSo, Kristin will need 10 x 54 = 540 hours to read half of her books.\nTherefore, Kristin will need 540/20 = 27 hours per book.\nTo read half of her books, Kristin will need 27 x 10 = 270 hours.\n 270\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Peter and Kristin are to read 20 fantasy novels each in a week. Peter can read three times as fast as Kristin. If Peter reads one book in 18 hours, how long will Kristin read half of her books?", "expected_answer": "540", "student_answer": "270", "student_confusion": "time", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-046", "mathdial_qid": 5000598, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "There were 672 balloons that were either green, blue, yellow, or red. They were divided into equal groups and then Anya took half of the yellow ones home. How many balloons did Anya take home?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nIf the balloons were divided into equal groups, we need to find the total number of balloons in each group. To do that, we can divide the total number of balloons by the number of groups:\n\n672 / 4 = 168 balloons in each group\n\nHalf of the yellow balloons in each group means:\n\n1/2 x (168 / 4) = 21 yellow balloons\n\nSo Anya took home 21 yellow balloons. \n\n 21\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "There were 672 balloons that were either green, blue, yellow, or red. They were divided into equal groups and then Anya took half of the yellow ones home. How many balloons did Anya take home?", "expected_answer": "84", "student_answer": "21", "student_confusion": "Was doing a further uneccessary division", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 1}
{"case_id": "mathdial-047", "mathdial_qid": 5000603, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Peter knows that the distance an airplane travels between Spain and Russia is 7019 km, while the distance between Spain and Germany is 1615 km. Peter took a flight from Spain, which made a stopover in Germany, while being there, he receives a call from his brother asking him how many kilometers he has to fly to get to Russia and then return to Spain.  What will Peter answer?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nPeter has to fly from Spain to Germany, which is 1615 km, and then from Germany to Russia, which is 7019 km, for a total of 1615 + 7019 = 8634 km.\nTo return to Spain, he has to fly another 7019 km, for a total distance of 8634 + 7019 = 15653 km.\nTherefore, Peter has to fly a total of 15653 km to get to Russia and return to Spain.\n 15653\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Peter knows that the distance an airplane travels between Spain and Russia is 7019 km, while the distance between Spain and Germany is 1615 km. Peter took a flight from Spain, which made a stopover in Germany, while being there, he receives a call from his brother asking him how many kilometers he has to fly to get to Russia and then return to Spain.  What will Peter answer?", "expected_answer": "12423", "student_answer": "15653", "student_confusion": "The student initially had a conceptual issue but then once they had worked out the correct sum they repeatedly made the same numerical error despite my telling them it was wrong. They did not respond to feudal.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 2}
{"case_id": "mathdial-048", "mathdial_qid": 5000614, "grade": 2, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Cindy can run at 3 miles per hour and walk at 1 mile per hour. If she runs for half a mile and then walks for half a mile, how many minutes will it take her to travel the full mile?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nCindy takes 1/3 hour to run half a mile (since speed = distance/time, time = distance/speed).\nSo, she takes 30 x 1/3 = 10 minutes to run half a mile.\nCindy takes 1 hour to walk 1 mile.\nSo, she takes 60 x 1 = 60 minutes to walk 1 mile.\nTherefore, it takes Cindy 10 + 60 + 10 = 80 minutes to travel the full mile by running and walking.\n 80\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Cindy can run at 3 miles per hour and walk at 1 mile per hour. If she runs for half a mile and then walks for half a mile, how many minutes will it take her to travel the full mile?", "expected_answer": "40", "student_answer": "80", "student_confusion": "The student wasn't dividing the time in the second half of the problem.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 1}
{"case_id": "mathdial-049", "mathdial_qid": 5000623, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Hooper Bay has twice as many pounds of lobster than the two other harbors combined. If the other two harbors have 80 pounds of lobster each, how many pounds of lobster are the three harbors holding?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThe two other harbors have a combined total of 80 x 2 = 160 pounds of lobster.\nHooper Bay has twice as much as the two other harbors combined, which means it has 160 x 2 = 320 pounds of lobster.\nAltogether, the three harbors are holding 160 + 160 + 320 = 640 pounds of lobster.\n 640\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Hooper Bay has twice as many pounds of lobster than the two other harbors combined. If the other two harbors have 80 pounds of lobster each, how many pounds of lobster are the three harbors holding?", "expected_answer": "480", "student_answer": "640", "student_confusion": "student mis-calculated in the last step by adding the amount of lobsters in both the harbors combined, twice.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-050", "mathdial_qid": 5000651, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "For the gala luncheon planning committee, Margaret wants to serve chicken salad sandwiches using mini croissants.  She wants there to be enough food that each person on the committee can have 2 sandwiches each.  Her bakery offers 12 minis croissants for $8.00.  There are 24 people on the committee. How much will she spend on croissants?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nMargaret needs to make 2 x 24 = 48 mini croissant sandwiches.\nThe bakery offers 12 mini croissants for $8.00, so each sandwich needs 2 croissants.\nThus, she needs 48 x 2 = 96 mini croissants.\nShe will need to buy 96/12 = 8 sets of 12 mini croissants.\nSo, she will spend 8 x $8.00 = $64.00 on croissants.\n 64.00\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "For the gala luncheon planning committee, Margaret wants to serve chicken salad sandwiches using mini croissants.  She wants there to be enough food that each person on the committee can have 2 sandwiches each.  Her bakery offers 12 minis croissants for $8.00.  There are 24 people on the committee. How much will she spend on croissants?", "expected_answer": "32", "student_answer": "64.00", "student_confusion": "The student was making an assumption about how many croissants they thought should be used per sandiwich and applying this to the problem.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 2}
{"case_id": "mathdial-051", "mathdial_qid": 5000657, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Mabel planted 4 tomato plants. One tomato plant bore 8 tomatoes and another bore 4 more tomatoes than the first. The two remaining plants each bore three times the number of tomatoes as the first two plants combined. How many tomatoes does Mabel have?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nOne tomato plant bore 8 tomatoes and another bore 4 more tomatoes than the first, so the second plant bore 8+4 = 12 tomatoes.\nThe first two plants bore 8+12 = 20 tomatoes combined.\nThe two remaining plants each bore 3 x 20 = 60 tomatoes.\nSo, Mabel has a total of 20+60 = 80 tomatoes.\n 80\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Mabel planted 4 tomato plants. One tomato plant bore 8 tomatoes and another bore 4 more tomatoes than the first. The two remaining plants each bore three times the number of tomatoes as the first two plants combined. How many tomatoes does Mabel have?", "expected_answer": "140", "student_answer": "80", "student_confusion": "Seth forgot the multiply 60 x 2", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
{"case_id": "mathdial-052", "mathdial_qid": 5000664, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Robie bought 3 bags of chocolates. She gave the 2 bags to her brothers and sisters then bought another 3 bags. How many bags of chocolates were left?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nRobie had 3 bags - 2 bags = 1 bag left after giving 2 bags to her brothers and sisters.\nThen she bought another 3 bags, so she had a total of 1 bag + 3 bags = 4 bags.\nTherefore, Robie had 4 bags - 2 bags = 2 bags of chocolates left after giving 2 bags to her brothers and sisters.\n 2\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Robie bought 3 bags of chocolates. She gave the 2 bags to her brothers and sisters then bought another 3 bags. How many bags of chocolates were left?", "expected_answer": "4", "student_answer": "2", "student_confusion": "The student accidentally subtracted twice from the beginning number of bags instead of just subtracting once.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 2}
{"case_id": "mathdial-053", "mathdial_qid": 5000683, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "The fall semester lasts 15 weeks.  During the weekdays, Paris studies 3 hours a day for her classes.  On the weekends, she spends 4 hours studying on Saturday and 5 hours studying on Sunday.  How much time does Paris study during the semester?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nParis studies a total of 5 weekdays x 3 hours/day = 15 hours per week.\nDuring the fall semester, she studies for a total of 15 weeks x 15 hours/week = 225 hours on weekdays.\nOn the weekends, she studies for a total of 4 + 5 = 9 hours.\nTherefore, Paris studies for a total of 225 + 9 = 234 hours during the semester.\n 234\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "The fall semester lasts 15 weeks.  During the weekdays, Paris studies 3 hours a day for her classes.  On the weekends, she spends 4 hours studying on Saturday and 5 hours studying on Sunday.  How much time does Paris study during the semester?", "expected_answer": "360", "student_answer": "234", "student_confusion": "Student did the sum in the wrong order, adding the weekend hours at the end instead of multiplying those also by 15", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
{"case_id": "mathdial-054", "mathdial_qid": 5000685, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "There are one-third as many Ford trucks as Dodge trucks in the parking lot of the Taco Castle.  But there are twice as many Ford trucks as Toyota trucks in this parking lot, and there are half as many Volkswagen Bugs as there are Toyota trucks in this same parking lot.  If there are 5 Volkswagon Bugs in the parking lot, how many Dodge trucks are in the parking lot of the Taco Castle?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nIf there are 5 Volkswagen Bugs in the parking lot, then there are twice as many Ford trucks as Toyota trucks, or 2 x 5 = 10 Ford trucks.\nAlso, there are half as many Volkswagen Bugs as there are Toyota trucks, or 5 x 2 = 10 Toyota trucks.\nTherefore, there are one-third as many Ford trucks as Dodge trucks, or 10 x 3 = 30 Dodge trucks in the parking lot. \n 30\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "There are one-third as many Ford trucks as Dodge trucks in the parking lot of the Taco Castle.  But there are twice as many Ford trucks as Toyota trucks in this parking lot, and there are half as many Volkswagen Bugs as there are Toyota trucks in this same parking lot.  If there are 5 Volkswagon Bugs in the parking lot, how many Dodge trucks are in the parking lot of the Taco Castle?", "expected_answer": "60", "student_answer": "30", "student_confusion": "The student miscalculated the number of Ford trucks in the first step", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-055", "mathdial_qid": 5000691, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "John takes 3 naps a week.  Each nap is 2 hours long.  In 70 days how many hours of naps does he take?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nJohn takes 3 x 2 = 6 hours of naps per week.\nIn 70 days, there are 10 weeks and 7 days left over.\nSo John takes 10 x 6 = 60 hours of naps in the 10 full weeks.\nAnd for the 7 remaining days, he takes 3 x 2 = 6 hours of naps.\nTherefore, John takes a total of 60 + 6 = 66 hours of naps in 70 days.\n 66\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "John takes 3 naps a week.  Each nap is 2 hours long.  In 70 days how many hours of naps does he take?", "expected_answer": "60", "student_answer": "66", "student_confusion": "Confused about the number of weeks in 70 days", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-056", "mathdial_qid": 5000699, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Camille goes to the Ice Cream Palace with her husband and two children. Each one orders a different ice cream. Camille orders a banana split, her husband orders a waffle bowl, her daughter orders a single cone, and her son orders a double cone. The cone has 1 scoop of ice cream, the banana split has 3 times as many scoops of ice cream as the cone and the waffle bowl has 1 more scoop than the banana split. How many scoops of ice cream did the ice cream man serve?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThe single cone has 1 scoop of ice cream.\nThe banana split has 3 times as many scoops of ice cream as the cone, so it has 3 x 1 = 3 scoops of ice cream.\nThe waffle bowl has 1 more scoop than the banana split, so it has 3 + 1 = 4 scoops of ice cream.\nAltogether, the ice cream man served 1 + 3 + 4 = 8 scoops of ice cream.\n 8\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Camille goes to the Ice Cream Palace with her husband and two children. Each one orders a different ice cream. Camille orders a banana split, her husband orders a waffle bowl, her daughter orders a single cone, and her son orders a double cone. The cone has 1 scoop of ice cream, the banana split has 3 times as many scoops of ice cream as the cone and the waffle bowl has 1 more scoop than the banana split. How many scoops of ice cream did the ice cream man serve?", "expected_answer": "10", "student_answer": "8", "student_confusion": "Student got last step wrong as he forgot to include the double cone.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
{"case_id": "mathdial-057", "mathdial_qid": 5000703, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Maggie has an after-school job that pays her $5.00 for every magazine subscription she can sell.  She sells 4 to her parents, 1 to her grandfather, 2 to the next-door neighbor and twice that amount to another neighbor.  How much money did Maggie earn?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nMaggie sold 4+1+2 = 7 magazine subscriptions to her family and neighbor.\nShe sold twice that amount, so she sold 2 x 7 = 14 magazine subscriptions to the other neighbor.\nIn total, she sold 7+14 = 21 magazine subscriptions.\nTherefore, Maggie earned 21 x $5.00 = $105.00\n 105.00\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Maggie has an after-school job that pays her $5.00 for every magazine subscription she can sell.  She sells 4 to her parents, 1 to her grandfather, 2 to the next-door neighbor and twice that amount to another neighbor.  How much money did Maggie earn?", "expected_answer": "55", "student_answer": "105.00", "student_confusion": "misunderstood the fact that maggie sold twice the amount of magazine subscription to another neighbour implies twice of what she sold to her next-door neighbour and not twice the total", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 2}
{"case_id": "mathdial-058", "mathdial_qid": 5000713, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Each week, Paul has 2 hours of homework on weeknights and 5 hours for the entire weekend. This week Paul has practice 2 nights out of the week and can't do any homework those nights. How many hours of homework does he have to average for the other nights to get his week's homework done?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nPaul has a total of 2 x 5 = 10 hours of homework for the entire week.\nHe has 2 x 2 = 4 hours of practice during the week.\nSo he has 7 - 2 = 5 nights to complete his homework.\nHe needs to average 10/5 = 2 hours of homework per night.\nTherefore, Paul needs to do 2 - 2 = 0 hours of homework on the nights he has practice and 2 hours of homework on the other 3 nights.\n 2\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Each week, Paul has 2 hours of homework on weeknights and 5 hours for the entire weekend. This week Paul has practice 2 nights out of the week and can't do any homework those nights. How many hours of homework does he have to average for the other nights to get his week's homework done?", "expected_answer": "3", "student_answer": "2", "student_confusion": "Student could not grasp that the weekly nights had to be multiplied by 2 and that had to be added onto 5 hours at the weekend.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
{"case_id": "mathdial-059", "mathdial_qid": 5000729, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "In five years, Rehana will be three times as old as Phoebe. If Rehana is currently 25 years old, and Jacob, Rehana's brother, is 3/5 of Phoebe's current age, how old is Jacob now?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nLet's start by finding how old Phoebe will be in five years when Rehana is three times as old. If Rehana is currently 25, in five years she will be 25+5 = 30 years old.\nIf in five years Rehana will be three times as old as Phoebe, Phoebe will be 1/3 of Rehana's age, which is 30/3 = 10 years old.\nIf Jacob is currently 3/5 of Phoebe's age, he is 3/5 x 10 = 6 years old.\n 6\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "In five years, Rehana will be three times as old as Phoebe. If Rehana is currently 25 years old, and Jacob, Rehana's brother, is 3/5 of Phoebe's current age, how old is Jacob now?", "expected_answer": "3", "student_answer": "6", "student_confusion": "The student forgot that Phoebe's age NOW needed to be found.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4, "beyond_class_5_note": "a division that does not come out whole"}
{"case_id": "mathdial-060", "mathdial_qid": 5000736, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Arabella is a dance student learning three new steps this session. Her instructor has her spend thirty minutes on learning the first step. The second step she masters in half the time. The third step is more complex, so it takes her as long as both the other steps to learn. How many minutes did she spend learning the three steps?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nArabella spent 30/2 = 15 minutes on the second step.\nShe spent 30 + 15 = 45 minutes on the first and second steps.\nIt took her 45 minutes to learn the first two steps.\nSo, she spent 45 x 2 = 90 minutes to learn the third step.\nTherefore, she spent 45 + 90 = 135 minutes learning all three steps.\n 135\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Arabella is a dance student learning three new steps this session. Her instructor has her spend thirty minutes on learning the first step. The second step she masters in half the time. The third step is more complex, so it takes her as long as both the other steps to learn. How many minutes did she spend learning the three steps?", "expected_answer": "90", "student_answer": "135", "student_confusion": "The student made a simple error . They were adding the 30 minutes twice", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-061", "mathdial_qid": 5000740, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "A road has four lanes, and in each lane, the number of cars is twice as many as the number of trucks in all the lanes. If there are 60 trucks in each lane, calculate the total number of vehicles in all the lanes?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nIf there are 60 trucks in each lane, and the number of cars is twice as many as the number of trucks in all the lanes, then there are 2*60 = 120 cars in each lane.\nThe total number of vehicles in one lane is the sum of the number of trucks and cars, which is 60+120 = 180.\nSince there are four lanes, the total number of vehicles in all the lanes is 180*4 = 720.\n 720\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "A road has four lanes, and in each lane, the number of cars is twice as many as the number of trucks in all the lanes. If there are 60 trucks in each lane, calculate the total number of vehicles in all the lanes?", "expected_answer": "2160", "student_answer": "720", "student_confusion": "The student doubled the number of trucks to get the number of cars before they had calculated the total number of trucks", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-062", "mathdial_qid": 5000774, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Chip takes 2 pages of notes every day, 5 days a week, for each of his 5 classes.  His notebook paper comes in packs of 100 sheets of paper per pack.  After 6 weeks, how many packs of notebook paper will Chip use?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nChip takes notes for 5 x 5 = 25 classes.\nHe takes 2 x 5 = 10 pages of notes every day.\nSo he takes 10 x 5 = 50 pages of notes every day for all his classes.\nHe takes 50 x 5 = 250 pages of notes every week.\nAfter 6 weeks, he takes 250 x 6 = 1500 pages of notes in total.\nTherefore, he needs 1500/100 = 15 packs of notebook paper.\n 15\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Chip takes 2 pages of notes every day, 5 days a week, for each of his 5 classes.  His notebook paper comes in packs of 100 sheets of paper per pack.  After 6 weeks, how many packs of notebook paper will Chip use?", "expected_answer": "3", "student_answer": "15", "student_confusion": "Student used the same number to repeat a calculation", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
{"case_id": "mathdial-063", "mathdial_qid": 5000794, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Bruno wants to buy two and one-half dozens of pens. How many pens will he have?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nOne dozen is equal to 12, so two dozens is equal to 2 x 12 = 24 pens.\nHalf of a dozen is equal to 6, so half of 12 is equal to 6 and half of a dozen is equal to 6.\nTherefore, one and a half dozen is equal to 1 x 12 + 6 = 18 pens.\nThus, two and one-half dozens of pens is equal to 24 + 18 = 42 pens.\n 42\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Bruno wants to buy two and one-half dozens of pens. How many pens will he have?", "expected_answer": "30", "student_answer": "42", "student_confusion": "student was not reading the information properly therefore kept consistantly getting the answer wrong.  i did make an error when breaking it down so on the last go i perhaps could have got him to solve it but i did not write the word half", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-064", "mathdial_qid": 5000810, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Tim takes his 3 children trick or treating.  They are out for 4 hours.  Each hour they visited 5 houses.  Each house gives 3 treats per kid.  How many treats do his children get in total?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nIn one hour, they visited 5 houses x 3 children = 15 houses.\nIn four hours, they visited 15 houses/hour x 4 hours = 60 houses.\nEach house gives 3 treats per child, so each house gives out 3 treats x 3 children = 9 treats.\nTherefore, the children get 60 houses x 9 treats/house = 540 treats in total.\n 540\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Tim takes his 3 children trick or treating.  They are out for 4 hours.  Each hour they visited 5 houses.  Each house gives 3 treats per kid.  How many treats do his children get in total?", "expected_answer": "180", "student_answer": "540", "student_confusion": "arithmatic error in the last step.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-065", "mathdial_qid": 5000817, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Henry needs to declutter his book collection of 99 books.  From his bookshelf, he was able to fill 3 boxes of 15 books each to take to a donation center.  He had 21 books in a room he could donate, 4 on his coffee table and 18 cookbooks stashed in the kitchen.  When he dropped them off, he noticed a box of books that had a  \"free to a good home\" note.  He grabbed 12 books to take back to his place.  How many books does Henry now have?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nHenry filled 3 boxes with a total of 3 x 15 = 45 books.\nIncluding the 21, 4, and 18 books, Henry donated a total of 21 + 4 + 18 = 43 books.\nTherefore, Henry had 99 - 43 = 56 books left before taking the 12 books from the \"free to a good home\" box.\nAfter taking the 12 books, Henry now has 56 + 12 = 68 books.\n 68\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Henry needs to declutter his book collection of 99 books.  From his bookshelf, he was able to fill 3 boxes of 15 books each to take to a donation center.  He had 21 books in a room he could donate, 4 on his coffee table and 18 cookbooks stashed in the kitchen.  When he dropped them off, he noticed a box of books that had a  \"free to a good home\" note.  He grabbed 12 books to take back to his place.  How many books does Henry now have?", "expected_answer": "23", "student_answer": "68", "student_confusion": "The student was adding a step at the end of the calculation that he didn't need. He did arrive at the correct answer, but then added another number to the calculation.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
{"case_id": "mathdial-066", "mathdial_qid": 5000818, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "A restaurant buffet has 36 different dishes available to try. The restaurant features mango salsa on three of its dishes, fresh mangoes in a sixth of its dishes, and mango jelly in one dish. Oliver despises mangoes and won't eat them, but can pick them out of two of the dishes with fresh mango that he would be willing to try. How many dishes are left for Oliver on the buffet?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThere are 3 + 36/6 + 1 = 10 dishes with mango in them.\nOliver won't eat 2 of the dishes with fresh mango, so there are 36/6 - 2 = 4 dishes with fresh mango he can try.\nThus, there are 10 - 2 - 4 = 4 dishes with mango he won't eat and dishes with fresh mango he won't try.\nTherefore, Oliver has 36 - 10 - 4 = 22 dishes left on the buffet. \n 22\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "A restaurant buffet has 36 different dishes available to try. The restaurant features mango salsa on three of its dishes, fresh mangoes in a sixth of its dishes, and mango jelly in one dish. Oliver despises mangoes and won't eat them, but can pick them out of two of the dishes with fresh mango that he would be willing to try. How many dishes are left for Oliver on the buffet?", "expected_answer": "28", "student_answer": "22", "student_confusion": "student misunderstood the initial information about the mango dishes that would be OK to eat", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
{"case_id": "mathdial-067", "mathdial_qid": 5000843, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Jimmy is a pizza delivery man. Each pizza costs 12 dollars and the delivery charge is 2 extra dollars if the area is farther than 1 km from the pizzeria. Jimmy delivers 3 pizzas in the park, which is located 100 meters away from the pizzeria. Also, he delivers 2 pizzas in a building 2 km away from the pizzeria. How much money did Jimmy get paid for the pizzas?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nFor the 3 pizzas delivered in the park, Jimmy only needs to charge 36 dollars (3 x 12). The distance is less than 1 km, so he doesn't need to add any delivery charges.\n\nFor the 2 pizzas delivered in the building, Jimmy needs to charge 24 dollars (2 x 12). The distance is farther than 1 km, so he needs to add 2 dollars for each pizza, which is a total of 4 dollars.\n\nSo the total amount of money Jimmy earned for the pizzas is 36 + 24 = 60 dollars.\n 60\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Jimmy is a pizza delivery man. Each pizza costs 12 dollars and the delivery charge is 2 extra dollars if the area is farther than 1 km from the pizzeria. Jimmy delivers 3 pizzas in the park, which is located 100 meters away from the pizzeria. Also, he delivers 2 pizzas in a building 2 km away from the pizzeria. How much money did Jimmy get paid for the pizzas?", "expected_answer": "64", "student_answer": "60", "student_confusion": "The student forgot to add the delivery charge for the 2 pizzas", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 1}
{"case_id": "mathdial-068", "mathdial_qid": 5000847, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Jimmy wants to play in the inflatable swimming pool in his backyard. The swimming pool is empty and the garage is locked, so he cannot use the hose to fill the pool. He finds a 2 gallon bucket and calculates that it takes 20 seconds to fill the bucket from the tap and carry it to the pool where he pours in the water. If the pool holds 84 gallons of water, how many minutes will it take Jimmy to fill the pool?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nTo fill the pool with 84 gallons of water, Jimmy needs to fill and carry 84/2 = 42 buckets of water.\nIt takes 20 seconds to fill and carry one bucket, so it takes 20 x 2 = 40 seconds to fill and carry two buckets.\nThus, Jimmy needs 42 x 40 = 1680 seconds to fill the pool.\nTo convert seconds to minutes, we divide by 60: 1680/60 = 28 minutes.\n 28\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Jimmy wants to play in the inflatable swimming pool in his backyard. The swimming pool is empty and the garage is locked, so he cannot use the hose to fill the pool. He finds a 2 gallon bucket and calculates that it takes 20 seconds to fill the bucket from the tap and carry it to the pool where he pours in the water. If the pool holds 84 gallons of water, how many minutes will it take Jimmy to fill the pool?", "expected_answer": "14", "student_answer": "28", "student_confusion": "Conceptual error in third step. calculated time for 2 trips and multiplied with total number of trips.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 1}
{"case_id": "mathdial-069", "mathdial_qid": 5000864, "grade": 3, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Carl has been selling watermelons on the side of the road for $3 each. This evening he went home with $105 in profit and 18 watermelons. How many watermelons did he start out with this morning?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nLet's start by finding out how much money Carl made selling the 18 watermelons. Since he sold each watermelon for $3, he made 18 x $3 = $54.\nWe know that his total profit for the day was $105, so he must have started with $105 - $54 = $51 worth of watermelons.\nSince each watermelon costs $3, he must have started with 51 / $3 = 17 watermelons.\n 17\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Carl has been selling watermelons on the side of the road for $3 each. This evening he went home with $105 in profit and 18 watermelons. How many watermelons did he start out with this morning?", "expected_answer": "53", "student_answer": "17", "student_confusion": "The student did not seem to grasp the concept", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 1}
{"case_id": "mathdial-070", "mathdial_qid": 5000866, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Kevin repairs phones at his job. At the beginning of the day, Kevin has 15 phones that need to be repaired. By the afternoon, Kevin has successfully repaired 3 of the 15 phones and a client has dropped off 6 more phones that need fixing. If a coworker of Kevin's offers to help him and fix half of the damaged phones, how many phones will each person need to repair?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nKevin has 15 - 3 = 12 phones left to repair in the afternoon.\nAfter the client drops off 6 more phones, Kevin has 12 + 6 = 18 phones to repair.\nKevin and his coworker will need to repair a total of 18 + 6 = 24 phones.\nIf Kevin's coworker will fix half of the phones, then each person will need to repair 24/2 = 12 phones.\n 12\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Kevin repairs phones at his job. At the beginning of the day, Kevin has 15 phones that need to be repaired. By the afternoon, Kevin has successfully repaired 3 of the 15 phones and a client has dropped off 6 more phones that need fixing. If a coworker of Kevin's offers to help him and fix half of the damaged phones, how many phones will each person need to repair?", "expected_answer": "9", "student_answer": "12", "student_confusion": "error in division of 18 by 2.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
