{"case_id": "mathdial-080", "mathdial_qid": 5001015, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "The library has 9,900 books. Over the summer, they sold some books. If only four sixths of the books are left, how many did they sell?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nFour sixths of the books that are left is equal to 2/6 of the original amount of books, since 6/6 - 4/6 = 2/6. \n\nSo, if 2/6 of the books are left, then 4/6 of the books were sold. \n\n4/6 of 9,900 books is: \n\n(4/6) x 9,900 = (2/3) x 9,900 \n\nTo simplify the multiplication, we can divide 9,900 by 3 first: \n\n3,300 x 2 = 6,600 \n\nSo the library sold 6,600 books. \n\n 6,600\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "The library has 9,900 books. Over the summer, they sold some books. If only four sixths of the books are left, how many did they sell?", "expected_answer": "3300", "student_answer": "6600", "student_confusion": "Student got confused with fractions and started contradicting himself. Also made a mistake in subtraction.", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-081", "mathdial_qid": 5001017, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "Chef Michel made shepherd's pie cut into 4 pieces and chicken pot pie cut into 5 pieces for the lunch crowd.  52 customers ordered slices of shepherd's pie and 80 customers ordered slices of chicken pot pie. How many total pies did Chef Michel sell?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nChef Michel made 4 x 52 = 208 slices of shepherd's pie.\nChef Michel made 5 x 80 = 400 slices of chicken pot pie.\nChef Michel sold 208/4 = 52 shepherd's pies.\nChef Michel sold 400/5 = 80 chicken pot pies.\nChef Michel sold 52+80 = 132 pies in total.\n 132\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "Chef Michel made shepherd's pie cut into 4 pieces and chicken pot pie cut into 5 pieces for the lunch crowd.  52 customers ordered slices of shepherd's pie and 80 customers ordered slices of chicken pot pie. How many total pies did Chef Michel sell?", "expected_answer": "29", "student_answer": "132", "student_confusion": "they were multiplying the number instead of dividing it", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 3}
{"case_id": "mathdial-082", "mathdial_qid": 5001022, "grade": 4, "question_type": "student_mistake", "question": "A hurricane is approaching the southern coast of Texas, and a rancher is planning to move 400 head of cattle 60 miles to higher ground to protect them from possible inland flooding that might occur.  His animal transport truck holds 20 head of cattle.  Traveling at 60 miles per hour, what is the total driving time, in hours, it will take to transport all of his cattle to higher ground?\n\nThis is how I did it:\nThe rancher needs to make 400/20 = 20 trips to move all the cattle.\nThe total distance the rancher will travel is 20 x 60 = 1200 miles.\nTo cover this distance at 60 miles per hour, he will need 1200/60 = 20 hours.\nTherefore, the total driving time to transport all the cattle will be 20 hours.\n 20\n\nIs my answer right?", "problem": "A hurricane is approaching the southern coast of Texas, and a rancher is planning to move 400 head of cattle 60 miles to higher ground to protect them from possible inland flooding that might occur.  His animal transport truck holds 20 head of cattle.  Traveling at 60 miles per hour, what is the total driving time, in hours, it will take to transport all of his cattle to higher ground?", "expected_answer": "40", "student_answer": "20", "student_confusion": "the student didn't account for the return trips needed by the farmer, and so underestimated the distance travelled", "accepted_equivalents": [], "verifiable": true, "no_forced_reveal": true, "solution_steps": 4}
